 <section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Retirees</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Retirement income: the clearest win of any state we compare</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ohio taxes pensions, 401(k) and IRA withdrawals at its flat rate. The relief is a retirement credit that tops out at $200 a year, one per return, and ends entirely at $100,000 of income, plus a $50 credit at 65.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">South Carolina works from deductions instead: up to $3,000 of retirement income before 65 and up to $10,000 from 65, per person. From 65 an age-65 deduction of up to $15,000 applies against any income, and the retirement deduction counts toward it. Social Security is untaxed in both states. For most retirees the same pension is simply taxed less here, and the property tax on the house drops at the same time.</p><div style="background:var(--ivory);border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:6px;padding:1.2rem 1.3rem;margin-top:1.8rem;max-width:720px;display:flex;gap:1rem;flex-wrap:wrap;align-items:center;justify-content:space-between"><p style="margin:0;color:var(--navy);font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.92rem;font-weight:600">Retiring here from Ohio? We will run your whole picture.</p><div style="display:flex;gap:.7rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><a class="btn btn-brass" href="#lead-form">Have an expert run my retirement math</a><a class="btn btn-outline" href="tel:+18543332135">Call 854.333.2135</a></div></div></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The other direction</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Two things cost more here, and Devin hit both</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">South Carolina taxes your car every year</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Ohio charges a flat registration of roughly $36 plus small local fees, and nothing based on the car&#39;s value. Here every vehicle is taxed yearly on its value. A $30,000 car is taxed roughly $360 outside the city limits and closer to $460 inside Myrtle Beach, plus a $50 road fee. The county bill must be paid before the plate renews. Arriving adds a one-time $250 fee per vehicle, with 45 days to register. A two-car household should plan on roughly $500 to $1,000 a year that did not exist in Ohio. The bill for boats and campers works the same way. Devin, who wrote this page, arrived from Ohio three years ago, and this tax was the item that surprised him: his first county bill was $325 for the car, plus the one-time $250 fee and the $50 road fee. The steps, in order, are on the <a href="/buyers/relocating/moving-checklist/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">new resident checklist</a>.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.15rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.4rem 0 .5rem">Houses and sales tax</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">A typical home here costs about $342,000 against about $254,000 in the Cleveland area, so the purchase itself is a step up for many Ohio buyers even though the tax rate on it is far lower. The figure is the Myrtle Beach metro; the towns around it price differently. If you are coming from a small town, you do not have to buy in a resort city. Conway, Longs, Aynor and Little River are all within a short drive of the beach. We are glad to show them next to the beachfront so you can compare. The <a href="/buyers/relocating/which-town/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">which town page</a> compares all of them properly. Sales tax runs 8 percent in the county and 9 percent in the city, against about 7.3 percent combined in Ohio. Groceries are untaxed in both.</p></div></section>
