@@ -141,7 +141,7 @@ document.addEventListener("keydown",function(e){if(e.key==="Escape"){w.classList
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The fact buyers miss</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Where storm water went: inland, days later</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">People shopping from out of state usually picture hurricane risk as an oceanfront problem. The last decade here says something different. Florence made landfall in North Carolina in September 2018, and the wind on the Grand Strand stayed below hurricane strength. The damage came from rain. It kept falling for days, and the Waccamaw River rose for almost two weeks, reaching a record 21.16 feet at Conway on September 26. Weather service records count about 1,000 flooded homes and businesses near the river, severe flooding along the Intracoastal Waterway in Socastee, and 1,580 more damaged homes in rural parts of the county. Two years earlier, Matthew had pushed the same river to what was then its record.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The question that matters when you buy is not how close the ocean is. A house two blocks from the beach on high ground and a house twelve miles inland beside the river carry different risks, and neither one is obvious from the photos of the home.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">That is what the maps are for. Every address has a FEMA flood zone, and the <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">insurance page</a> explains why the zone named X still does not mean zero risk. Every address also has a county evacuation zone, covered next.</p></div></section>
 
-<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">After the rain</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Streets here drain fast, and there is a reason</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Devin, who wrote this page, remembers noticing it during Tropical Storm Debby. There were days of rain, a few inches of water standing on some streets, and driving was slow but possible. Then the main roads dried almost as soon as the rain stopped. He moved here from Ohio and expected the water to sit. On the main roads, it did not.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Part of the reason is built and paid for. Myrtle Beach has spent close to $75 million on four deepwater ocean outfalls. These are large underground pipes that collect street runoff and carry it more than 1,000 feet offshore, past the breakers. That keeps it from standing in town or pouring across the sand. North Myrtle Beach has built six of its own since 2002 and states the purpose plainly: better inland drainage keeps streets and properties dry, and moving the discharge offshore keeps bacteria out of the surf where people swim. More outfalls are planned in both cities.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">When heavy rain lands on a high tide, the drains have nowhere to send the water for a few hours. The lowest streets in places like Cherry Grove and Garden City hold water until the tide drops. River flooding is the opposite of street flooding: near the Waccamaw and the Intracoastal, water rises over days and stays, as the section above describes. Fast-draining streets are a beach-town fact, not a county-wide one.</p></div></section>
+<section style="background:var(--ivory)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">After the rain</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Streets here drain fast, and there is a reason</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">One of our team remembers noticing it during Tropical Storm Debby. There were days of rain, a few inches of water standing on some streets, and driving was slow but possible. Then the main roads dried almost as soon as the rain stopped. He moved here from Ohio and expected the water to sit. On the main roads, it did not.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Part of the reason is built and paid for. Myrtle Beach has spent close to $75 million on four deepwater ocean outfalls. These are large underground pipes that collect street runoff and carry it more than 1,000 feet offshore, past the breakers. That keeps it from standing in town or pouring across the sand. North Myrtle Beach has built six of its own since 2002 and states the purpose plainly: better inland drainage keeps streets and properties dry, and moving the discharge offshore keeps bacteria out of the surf where people swim. More outfalls are planned in both cities.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">When heavy rain lands on a high tide, the drains have nowhere to send the water for a few hours. The lowest streets in places like Cherry Grove and Garden City hold water until the tide drops. River flooding is the opposite of street flooding: near the Waccamaw and the Intracoastal, water rises over days and stays, as the section above describes. Fast-draining streets are a beach-town fact, not a county-wide one.</p></div></section>
 
 <section style="background:var(--ivory-2)"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">No storm required</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">King tides, and the few low streets they reach</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A king tide is the informal name for the highest tides of the year. They arrive on predictable dates, mostly around fall full moons, with no storm attached. On those days salt water can come up through the storm drains on the lowest streets until the tide turns.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Pawleys Island is the local example. On the biggest tides the island&#39;s low streets flood, and the causeways have been closed at the peak until the water dropped. The town says so itself: officials call flooding their first public safety issue, and the island is funding a drainage and flood plan. People who live there check the dates and plan around them. If you are shopping on Pawleys, the tide calendar comes with the island; ask us and we will time a showing to a high tide so you see the street at its worst before you offer.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Cherry Grove and the lowest streets of Garden City see the same tides.</p></div></section>
 
