 <section id="trash" style="background:var(--ivory-2);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">The one nobody warns you about</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Outside the city limits, no truck comes for your trash</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, trash is easy: about $34 a month on the same utility statement as water and sewer. That buys a garbage cart, a recycling cart, and two cubic yards of bulk and yard waste a week, all collected the same day. Carts go out no earlier than 6 p.m. the night before and come in by 11 p.m. on collection day, and that is a code the city enforces. Yard waste has to be in paper bags, not plastic.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In unincorporated Horry County there is no curbside collection at all. The county solid waste authority says so plainly, and nothing starts automatically when you close. You have three options: haul your own to one of the 25 recycling and convenience centers, hire a private hauler, or rely on a neighborhood association contract that covers it. The centers are open to unincorporated residents only and cap you at four cubic yards a day. Ask which of those three applies to the specific address before you close. People move here from cities and discover this on the second weekend.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">One more line item: sales tax runs 9 percent inside the City of Myrtle Beach and 8 percent everywhere else in Horry County. Unprepared groceries are taxed at zero either way.</p></div></section>
 
-<section id="parking" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Free beach parking</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">The resident parking decal, and who qualifies</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The City of Myrtle Beach gives residents a free parking decal that covers every city-owned paid space: the on-street meters and pay stations, the street-end beach accesses, the decal-only areas in The Avenues, and events at the convention center. It does not cover the Pavilion garage or any private lot. What you can do on the sand once you park, town by town, is on our <a href="/buyers/relocating/beaches/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">beach rules page</a>. Given that beach access parking runs $3 an hour or $15 for the day in season, it is worth doing in your first month. Devin lived here more than a year before he learned the decal existed.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fewer people qualify than assume they do. It is not for all Horry County residents. It is for vehicles that live inside the city limits and on which <em>city</em> vehicle property tax has been paid. The city treats that tax as your parking payment. If you live in Carolina Forest or Socastee or Surfside, you do not qualify, no matter how close you are.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Apply in person the first time, at the parking office in the Pavilion Parking Garage, 914 North Kings Highway, at the Ninth Avenue North entrance; the number is 843-626-7275 and hours are worth a call first. Renewals can be done online. Bring, for each vehicle, your South Carolina registration, your driver&#39;s license, and the current Horry County vehicle tax receipt, and note that the address on the license has to match the address on the tax receipt. Golf cart owners bring the state permit number too. The decal runs two years and replacements cost $20.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">The other towns work differently and the details do not transfer. North Myrtle Beach has no physical decal at all; enforcement reads your license plate, registration renews every year, and its paid season runs March through October, 9 to 5. Myrtle Beach&#39;s paid season is shorter, March through September, but runs 9 a.m. to midnight. North Myrtle Beach does sell a limited number of annual passes to Horry County residents who live outside its limits, but the allocation is small and sells out early in the year, so it is not something to count on.</p></div></section>
