 <section id="golfcart" style="background:var(--ivory);scroll-margin-top:120px"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Getting around</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;letter-spacing:-.01em">Golf cart rules, because this is a golf cart town</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Many neighborhoods here run on golf carts, and the rules changed in 2025. To drive one on a public road you need four things. A $5 state permit from the DMV, good for five years or until you move. Proof of ownership and liability insurance. A driver who is at least 16 with a valid license. The registration and insurance in the cart. Statewide, a permitted cart may run in daylight, on roads posted 35 mph or less, within four miles of the address on the permit. In a gated community the four miles are measured from the gate. It may cross a faster road at an intersection. Every passenger under 12 must be belted.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Towns can add rules, and three of them have. North Myrtle Beach allows carts sunrise to sunset on 35 mph roads and lets them cross, but not drive along, Ocean Boulevard, 27th Avenue South, Highway 17, and Sea Mountain Highway; the fine is $100 and up to 30 days. Surfside Beach is the exception on hours: carts with permanently mounted headlights and taillights may run at night, carts without lights only from 30 minutes before sunrise to 30 minutes after sunset. The City of Myrtle Beach keeps daylight hours, bars carts from sidewalks and multi-use paths, and closes Ocean Boulevard to carts over Memorial Day weekend. Outside city limits the state rules apply.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px">Before you buy in a neighborhood because it is &ldquo;golf cart friendly,&rdquo; check the posted speed limits on the roads between the house and wherever you plan to drive. A 45 mph road you cannot cross at an intersection ends the trip.</p></div></section>
 
